 "asked_at": "2026-10-17T03:42+08:00",
 "answer": "I can't see a live feed, so these figures come from search snapshots of air quality sites. Some of the snapshots are undated or old, so they may not match this minute.\n\n**Penang overall**\n- **Weather Underground:** AQI 68, \"Moderate\", with PM2.5 as the main pollutant. It advises people with respiratory problems to limit prolonged or heavy outdoor exertion.\n- **AccuWeather (Penang International Airport):** AQI 67. It labels this \"Poor\" on its own scale, which is stricter than the US scale.\n\n**Local stations (IQAir)**\n- **Bukit Mertajam:** US AQI 58, Moderate, with PM2.5 at about 12.9 µg/m³.\n- **Tanjung Tokong:** US AQI 79, Moderate.\n- **George Town:** US AQI 125, Unhealthy for sensitive groups. This reading looked older.\n- **Batu Feringgi:** US AQI 159, Unhealthy. This reading was also older.\n\n**Summary:** Air quality in Penang looks to be in the Moderate range, roughly AQI 55–80, with PM2.5 as the main pollutant. Acceptable for most people, but sensitive groups (asthma, heart or lung conditions, children, older adults) may want to limit long, strenuous time outdoors. Levels vary by location and have been higher at times, for example in George Town and Batu Feringgi. Haze episodes, often from regional smoke or open burning, can push readings up quickly.\n\nFor a true real-time reading, check [IQAir](https://www.iqair.com/air-quality/malaysia/penang) or the Malaysian Department of Environment's APIMS (Air Pollutant Index Management System). Malaysia's official API uses a different scale from the US AQI.",
